{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on evidence-only Draft1, primary5aee80549b03f341536441715d882ace78d09b3d at immutable attachmentcommit05ec599151a61d4af8c796c31847fb93d460ac4f, path attachments/2026-10-03-attention-runtime-handoff-draft1.md. Root read all66 split lines,10,054bytes, and verified SHA256c25e253835b449bc22dc9469b9cb15210476a3ad58020de52ec71a2f24cf8098. This is design evidence for03d85a35, not source/public-schema approval or implementation closure. The higher-priority overhead verdict3965e230 was filed first.\n\nP2 — Decide how changed visibility catches up. Draft lines21–22 always resume from one persisted attention cursor, and record dispositions for work outside the current task filter. At pinned e6, reads.ts:207–209 derives current member/teams/role, while attentionItems:230 requires pos greater than the saved position. core.ts:976–977 stores actual role:admin rows; policy notifications:1600–1601 can retain a team principal. admission.ts:1421–1423 and1479–1481 changes role/team without reissuing older rows. Policy role targets separately expand to member handles at evaluation, so do not generalize this case to every role-target notification. Current principal/work-filter changes can reveal an open item below the cursor; an ordinary incremental page is not a full view of newly eligible work. This corroborates planner's b5471b77 source-derived case, not an observed production failure or an API contract violation.\n\nDraft2 should state a bounded, resumable fresh/current-state catch-up under C3 when role/team/work selection changes, reusing existing durable per-item identities and current authority checks. Reconsider dispositions skipped solely by the former filter; keep already submitted/completed input identities deduplicated. Budget exhaustion must leave the remaining catch-up visible and resumable. If earlier work is intentionally excluded, name that exclusion as an explicit reviewed contract choice instead of silently claiming complete current work. Keep fresh-scan progress separate from ordinary acknowledged progress so a catch-up page cannot regress the normal cursor. Public field encoding remains unadopted.\n\nAdd one focused acceptance scenario: an open team item is at position10 before the Agent joins the team; a direct item at20 is retained and its cursor saved; joining makes10 visible without reissuing it. Catch-up retains/submits10 once, restart/repeated catch-up reuses that identity, and revocation before dispatch prevents new mutating tools. Include the configured-work-filter version without creating a cross-product quota.\n\nTwo related implementation clarifications should remain explicit in the handoff. First, line22's atomic ledger/cursor commit alone does not order overlapping readers. Serialize ingestion or check its saved starting cursor/selection generation in the same commit before advancement. A late smaller or obsolete page must not regress progress or overwrite newer selection state; stale work can be retried using the same identities. This belongs to the existing C3 durable-input owner, not a new Room cursor schema or another implementation lane. Extend the page/restart scenario with overlapping drainers rather than commissioning more tests by field.\n\nSecond, preserve the adopted prompt-provenance rule at line23's conversation submission. The wake note at337a449d:219–222 requires text from another member to be attributed data from that author, not Room authority. Durable retention/retry must preserve that distinction. Current pi Agent.run:527–530 forwards caller content/requestId; its completed-retry fixture:28–43 is caller driven, and README:174–182 explicitly leaves autonomous alarms and the attention bridge unfinished. That fixture does not demonstrate an actual receiver preserving provenance. Require the C3 receiver's eventual evidence at its real storage/submission boundary; no new public act field or runtime vulnerability is asserted here.\n\nArchitecture: The proposed local pending-input transaction and stable conversation identity correctly bridge Room and conversation durability without a cross-object transaction or second public SDK outbox. Exclusive queue positions, separate attention/conversation/update cursor kinds and later items about older entries match actual reads. A wake is a hint; it cannot acknowledge items or resume paused work. Name a bounded active alarm/catch-up path, retaining pause/attention-waiting inputs and allowing browser-disconnected active work as already commissioned.\n\nSecurity: Input delivery, attention closure, act replay and uncertain command settlement remain separate. Delayed input must use current authority/lease/epoch state. One revoked owner device versus removed owner/last active key remains the C1 distinction; settlement is not a fresh grant. Keep because eligibility and automatic review/check closure as a pending contract decision under the existing wake/C1 owners, not an implication that a citing note meets a formal obligation or that conversation submission finishes addressed work.\n\nSimplification: C3 owns the private ingestion/catch-up mechanics, C1 owns reviewed authority/lifecycle integration, the wake owner owns public cursor meaning and because eligibility, N2 owns requester acceptance, and N7 owns human notifications. The note does not reset the adopted schedule defaults/staging at337a449d:268–275. HMAC wake addresses, schedules and application-pack prompts are not invented prerequisites for the already commissioned alarm/input loop. Amend this dated clarification and its compact acceptance scenarios; leave frozen005/006 evidence intact and keep the user's full scope and builder-owned Jam judgment.\n\nVerification was a full frozen-note/hash read, pinned Room and pi source reads, existing commissioning decisions and a scoped parallel source review. No runtime suite, source edit, provider operation, new implementation request or Jam gate was performed or required. Existing five scenarios remain useful; they are not a cap. Return a revised immutable Draft2 that makes the visibility choice, ingestion ordering and inherited provenance requirement explicit.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5aee80549b03f341536441715d882ace78d09b3d","binding":"evidence-only","head":"05ec599151a61d4af8c796c31847fb93d460ac4f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:32171d4def61c5e7f831054f085abf19166c24fd\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b5471b7717d0464486a47c2bac763f0199730802\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3965e2309485f2e2bfb4bdc576355c521e531fa6","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}